// PLAN-1123: the AV/Rust stdlib `tls` contract face — the public API record
// of the EXPLICIT secure transport (the 974 r2 optional provider path). One
// exchange = one bounded byte exchange: connect (direct, or through an HTTP
// proxy with CONNECT), a VERIFIED TLS handshake, write the request bytes,
// read the response bytes to end of stream, close. The provider is a byte
// transport only — HTTP request formation and response parsing stay in Auto,
// so the public HTTP request/response contract (status*10000+body_len over
// the request bytes the caller built) is unchanged.
//
// `spec` is a block of `key value` lines, one empty line, then the request
// bytes. Every field is read from THIS exchange — nothing is carried over
// from an earlier one (no stale authorization, proxy setting or socket):
//   host <dns-name | ip | [v6]>   required; also the verified server name
//   port <1..65535>               required
//   trust file:<path> | system    required for https; there is NO insecure
//                                source (verification cannot be turned off)
//   via <host:port>               optional; the HTTP proxy
//   mode https | http             optional, default https; `http` is plain
//                                bytes through a forwarding proxy (requires
//                                `via`; the caller writes the absolute-form
//                                request line)
//   timeout <ms>                  optional, default 1500, at most 60000
//
// Ownership: `tls_exchange` opens an exchange the app owns; the socket is
// closed before the call returns, so the handle only owns the response
// bytes. `tls_result` is the outcome, `tls_read_byte` walks the response
// (0..255, then -1 at its end), `tls_close` releases it. A closed or stale
// handle is rejected before the callee runs (the owned model).
//
// Outcomes (`tls_result`): 0 = clean end of stream; a negative named value:
//   -1 tls.spec        malformed spec (unknown/duplicate key, bad value, no trust source)
//   -2 net.resolve     host or proxy could not be resolved
//   -3 net.connect     TCP connect refused/failed
//   -4 net.timeout     the deadline expired (connect, CONNECT, handshake, exchange)
//   -5 proxy.rejected  the proxy answered CONNECT with a non-2xx status
//   -6 proxy.closed    the proxy closed before a CONNECT verdict
//   -7 proxy.bad       the proxy's CONNECT reply had no parseable status
//   -8 tls.trust       the trust source is missing, unreadable or empty
//   -9 tls.untrusted   the peer chains to an issuer outside the trust source
//   -10 tls.hostname   the peer certificate is not valid for `host`
//   -11 tls.cert       another certificate validation failure
//   -12 tls.handshake  TLS protocol failure, or the peer closed mid-handshake
//   -13 net.write      writing the request failed
//   -14 net.read       reading the response failed
//   -15 net.truncated  the peer closed without close_notify (bytes kept)
//   -16 net.limit      the request exceeds 64 KiB, or the response 1 MiB (bytes kept)
// A verification failure happens BEFORE any request byte is written.
//
// One exchange is one foreign call: the execution wall budget of the
// running program (`ExecLimits.wall`, 2 s by default) must exceed the
// request's `timeout`, or the executor's wall-budget outcome preempts the
// named `net.timeout`. The default stays below that budget on purpose; a
// longer `timeout` is the caller's explicit choice for a host that raised
// the wall.
pub spec fn tls_exchange(spec str) int
pub spec fn tls_result(exchange int) int
pub spec fn tls_read_byte(exchange int) int
pub spec fn tls_close(exchange int) int
